WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.608
Fair warning.

2
00:00:01.628 --> 00:00:04.551
This is an embellished
fictional dramatization

3
00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:07.432
that may or may not be
based on true stories.

4
00:00:07.452 --> 00:00:10.062
The narrator is a guy named Charlie Hack.

5
00:00:10.082 --> 00:00:12.942
Any resemblance to me
is mostly coincidental.

6
00:00:12.962 --> 00:00:17.764
Names, characters, companies and events
are fictitious, or used fictitiously.

7
00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:19.996
Silicon Valley C Players.

8
00:00:20.016 --> 00:00:23.585
This preview jumps into
the middle of the book.

9
00:00:23.605 --> 00:00:27.100
Part Three. C Things That
Looked Like A Things.

10
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.274
Silicon Valley is very good at
telling a story about A players.

11
00:00:30.294 --> 00:00:33.052
The people who look like they
were always going to win.

12
00:00:33.072 --> 00:00:36.028
The companies that look like
they were always going to work.

13
00:00:36.048 --> 00:00:39.750
The myth is a straight line: a
garage, a genius, a ticker symbol.

14
00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.401
Most of the real work
looks nothing like that.

15
00:00:42.421 --> 00:00:46.180
It looks like another ordinary
Tuesday, doing the next right thing.

16
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.933
From the outside, a C thing
can look like an A thing.

17
00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:52.436
Applause is loud. The
real work is quieter.

18
00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:56.644
The book is about that gap: what people
saw, and what was really going on.

19
00:00:56.664 --> 00:00:58.363
A C player is not a failure.

20
00:00:58.383 --> 00:01:01.446
A C player is the person
who was never the smartest

21
00:01:01.466 --> 00:01:04.292
in the room, and kept going anyway.

22
00:01:04.312 --> 00:01:07.079
There's always someone
smarter, richer, wiser.

23
00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:08.620
Stay humble. Stay curious.

24
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.890
The Valley falls in love with products.

25
00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.648
Specific wins. Specific years.

26
00:01:12.668 --> 00:01:16.393
I got more out of building a
platform: a foundation you can stand

27
00:01:16.413 --> 00:01:19.684
on, and build the next thing
on, and the thing after that.

28
00:01:19.704 --> 00:01:22.068
You don't invent a career in one move.

29
00:01:22.088 --> 00:01:25.063
You stack building blocks.
Habits. Fundamentals.

30
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:27.875
The unglamorous layer
nobody puts on a slide.

31
00:01:27.895 --> 00:01:30.748
That layer is what you
actually stand on later.

32
00:01:30.768 --> 00:01:36.137
It's all relative, how good
you look from the outside.

33
00:01:36.157 --> 00:01:39.676
I just kept going. And
I kept doing my best.

34
00:01:39.696 --> 00:01:44.696
Then the work itself changed. I.

35
00:01:44.716 --> 00:01:47.404
Which is funny, because
I should be the saddest.

36
00:01:47.424 --> 00:01:51.161
I. came after the code writers first. I.

37
00:01:51.181 --> 00:01:53.284
now writes essentially all of the code.

38
00:01:53.304 --> 00:02:04.545
So gear your software engineering
practices around that.

39
00:02:04.565 --> 00:02:04.660
I.

40
00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.894
The book begins with a
green-screen dream. I.

41
00:02:08.914 --> 00:02:11.811
This part, in the middle, is the turn.

42
00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.070
It's free. It's open.

43
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.931
And when version one is
ready, if you were around

44
00:02:15.951 --> 00:02:19.020
for any of it, you get to tell your side.

45
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:20.908
The story is still being written.

46
00:02:20.928 --> 00:02:24.652
Silicon Valley C Players. Coming in 2027.
